 CO4.1  ALLOCATION SUMMARY
==============================================================================
   Method                  Placed   Used   Free   Util %   Meta (B)  Internal (B)
   Contiguous                   8    477     23     95.4          0         2,692
   Linked                       8    477     23     95.4      1,908         2,692
   Indexed                      8    485     15     97.0     32,768         2,692
   Extent (ext4-style)          8    477     23     95.4          0         2,692

   Internal fragmentation is identical for every method (it is a
   property of the block size, not the allocator). Metadata overhead
   is where the methods genuinely differ.

==============================================================================
 CO4.2  ACCESS COST - block I/Os for sequential vs random reads
==============================================================================

   nginx-access.log  (60 blocks, sequential-access workload)
   Method                   Sequential I/Os   Random I/Os   Random penalty
   Contiguous                            60            15             1.0x
   Linked                                60           435            29.0x
   Indexed                               61            16             1.1x
   Extent (ext4-style)                   60            15             1.0x

   tenant-db.dump  (95 blocks, random-access workload)
   Method                   Sequential I/Os   Random I/Os   Random penalty
   Contiguous                            95            23             1.0x
   Linked                                95         1,035            45.0x
   Indexed                               96            24             1.0x
   Extent (ext4-style)                   95            23             1.0x

   Linked allocation is the outlier by two orders of magnitude on
   random access, because reaching block i costs i pointer hops and
   there is no way to shortcut the chain. For tenant-db.dump this is
   the difference between 23 I/Os and 1,035 -- a 45x penalty.

