 CO4.2  EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION UNDER CREATE/DELETE CHURN
==============================================================================
   Free-space map after the initial contiguous allocation
   ('#' = allocated, '.' = free)
      0| ####################################################################################################
    100| ####################################################################################################
    200| ####################################################################################################
    300| ####################################################################################################
    400| #############################################################################.......................
   Free 23 blocks, largest run 23, external fragmentation 0.000

   After deleting syslog-2026-09.log, tenant-db.dump, guest-ubuntu.img:
      0| ##........................................##########################################################
    100| ##...............................................................................................###
    200| ####################################################################################################
    300| #################...................................................................................
    400| .......######################################################################.......................
   Free 248 blocks, largest run 95, external fragmentation 0.617
   Free extents: [(2, 40), (102, 95), (317, 90), (477, 23)]

   Now create rebuild-2026.vmdk requiring 150 blocks:
     Contiguous            FAILED     REFUSED: needs 150 contiguous, largest run is 95
     Linked                ALLOCATED  head=2, tail=331, 150 next-pointers (600 B stolen from data)
     Indexed               ALLOCATED  index block(s)=[4], 150 data blocks
     Extent (ext4-style)   ALLOCATED  2 extent(s): [102..196], [317..371]

   The disk holds 248 free blocks -- comfortably more
   than the 150 requested -- yet contiguous allocation
   refuses the request because no single run is large enough. That is
   external fragmentation stated as a measurement rather than a
   definition, and it is the failure mode that rules contiguous
   allocation out for a platform with constant VM churn.

==============================================================================
 CO4.1  RECOMMENDATION BY FILE CLASS
==============================================================================

   (a) Config files  < 4 KB
     examples       : named.conf, dhcpd.conf, resolv.conf
     RECOMMENDATION : Inline / direct blocks in the inode
     justification  : One block, one I/O. Indexing costs a whole extra 4 KB block for a 2 KB file -- 200% overhead. Modern ext4 inlines such files in the inode's 60-byte i_block area, so the read costs zero data I/Os.

   (b) Web logs 10-100 MB
     examples       : nginx-access.log, syslog
     RECOMMENDATION : Indexed (single + double indirect)
     justification  : Append-heavy and read sequentially, but occasionally grepped at random offsets. Indexed gives O(1) random reach for ~0.1% metadata overhead, and the file grows without needing contiguous space.

   (c) VM images  > 50 GB
     examples       : guest-win11.vmdk, guest-ubuntu.img
     RECOMMENDATION : Extent-based (ext4/XFS extents)
     justification  : A 50 GB file is 13,107,200 blocks; a classical index would need 12,814 metadata blocks. Four extents describe the same file in 48 bytes inside the inode, and keep the physical layout sequential for streaming reads.

